    "result_text": "Build succeeds and the test passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in the existing `TelephoneNormalizer` \u2014 the class that already owns telephone normalization/validation, so the rule lives where it belongs rather than being duplicated in the controller.\n\n**Change** (`src/main/java/.../rest/validation/TelephoneNormalizer.java`):\n\n- Added a `NATIONAL_LENGTH_BY_COUNTRY` map declaring the required national-number length per recognised country code (`\"61\"` \u2192 9, `\"1\"` \u2192 10).\n- Refactored `normalize` to separate the **country code** from the **national number** (instead of lumping all digits together): for `+`-prefixed input it detects the recognised country code via a small `recognisedCountryCode` helper (longest match wins); for input without a country code it defaults to `61` and drops a single leading `0`.\n- After the existing generic 8\u201315 digit check, it now rejects with `InvalidTelephoneException` (\u2192 HTTP 400 via the existing exception advice) when the national number's length doesn't match what the country requires.\n- Unrecognised country codes fall back to the prior generic behaviour, so nothing else regresses.\n\nThe new acceptance test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`+61 123` \u2192 400) passes, and `uniqueTelephone()` (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 9 national digits under `+61`) still validates, keeping the rest of the suite green.",
